Subject: [PATCH 39/39] Keep the decoder's dma-buf open until the presenter copies it

Keep the decoder's dma-buf open until the presenter copies it

A compositor handoff exports a descriptor: the plane offsets, the strides and
the dma-buf fds the decoder opened for them, and the export already owns its
own F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC duplicate of the decoder's handle. That is not the whole
story: a compositor handoff is queued, not completed. The presenter sends the
add and create_immed pair and the compositor imports the planes only when that
message reaches it, and this crate's CPU copy maps the same fds when it runs
behind the decoder. A retired surface makes the import fail ("Failed to import
plane 0 as R8" followed by a protocol error which closes the stream window) and
makes the copy fail with "Bad file descriptor (os error 9)".

The producer keeps the decoder frames whose descriptor went out to the
compositor alive over a bounded window, oldest first, and the ring is what
retires one: the surface, and the fds its export named, stay alive until the
queued copy and the compositor import have read them. Nothing is duplicated
beyond the export's own duplicate and nothing is closed here: the retention is
an extra reference to the frame the producer already owns, so the exported
descriptors stay exactly the ones the decoder exported and the application's
descriptor count is unchanged.

The window's width is measured, not assumed, and 24 was wrong. Pinning 24
frames pins the whole capture pool v4l2 request recycles, so the decoder stops
reusing its surfaces and names a fresh DMA-BUF for every frame; the presenter
keeps one compositor buffer per DMA-BUF object, so that cache misses every
frame, the compositor EGL-imports a new image every frame instead of reusing
the small set it reused in the 55 fps runs, and the driver eventually refuses
one of those imports and answers the fatal "importing the supplied dmabufs
failed" (error 7), which closed the stream window about a second after it
opened. The default here is one pinned frame: enough to keep the surface and
the fds it named alive across both handoffs, and enough to leave the pool
recycling. OPENNOW_HANDOFF_RETAINED=N overrides the default for the A/B runs,
clamped to 1..=24 so the decoder is never asked to hold more surfaces than it
had before this change.

The route chooser in this file then had a second problem. The interface
describes the import contract it enabled, and an interface that adopts a device
this crate created reports nothing at all: the context log reads
"dmabuf_image_import=false dmabuf_buffer_import=false", and that report alone
sends every DMA-BUF frame through the CPU copy, a three-megabyte memcpy of the
NV12 planes per present that costs a fifth of the rate the decoder produced.
Ask the device instead of the report. The first linear NV12 frame attempts the
VkBuffer import, whose own probe of the device's external-buffer properties is
already in sand::import_linear, and the answer - not the report - decides the
rest of the run: a device that cannot import keeps taking the copy it took
before, and one that can keeps the decoded NV12 on the GPU and lets the render
pass sample the decoder's own planes. Only the buffer bit is derived this way.
The image import contract, which asks the driver for a DMA-BUF image through
the DRM format modifier extensions this one does not offer, stays as the
interface reports it.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]
